    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Miologists need to **learn** about each phase to truly **help** students visualize how cells replicate. In prophase, chromosomes condense and the nuclear envelope breaks down before they can be seen clearly. During metaphase, chromosomes line up in the center to ensure they will be distributed equally. In anaphase, sister chromatids pull apart, and in telophase, two new nuclei begin to form as chromosomes relax. Finally, cytokinesis splits the cell into two identical daughter cells, completing the cycle. By following these steps, learners will **understand** how organic matter grows and repairs itself safely.",
      "source": "target",
